        <h2 id="damp-vs-waterproof" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Damp Proofing vs Waterproofing
        </h2>

        <p>
          These two terms get used interchangeably in marketing and mean genuinely different things in practice, and the gap between them explains a lot of surprised homeowners.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Damp proofing</strong> resists moisture vapour and light seepage. The thin spray-on asphalt coating applied to the outside of many new foundations before backfilling is damp proofing. It is a code minimum in many places, it is not rated for liquid water under pressure, and it degrades over the decades. Plenty of people discover a wet basement and assume the foundation was never treated; usually it was, with a product that was never intended to hold back water.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Waterproofing</strong> is rated to resist liquid water under hydrostatic pressure — the force water exerts when saturated soil presses against a wall. Elastomeric membranes and cementitious coatings with published psi ratings sit in this category. The distinction matters when you are reading a product label: "water resistant" and "damp proofing" are not the same claim as a pressure rating.
        </p>

        <p>
          A third category, <strong>penetrating sealers</strong>, sidesteps the surface entirely by reacting inside the concrete. They do not carry pressure ratings in the same way, because there is no coating to be pushed off — the capillaries themselves are blocked.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Four Numbers That Decide It</p>
