        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs Your Foundation Wants Sealing</h3>
        <p>
          Five symptoms point at moisture moving through the wall rather than around it, and all five are the case sealing genuinely solves.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A musty smell that never clears.</strong> The most reliable early sign, and the one people stop noticing because it arrives gradually. Moisture evaporating off masonry into a poorly ventilated space feeds the mould and mildew responsible for the odour.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>White chalky bloom on the walls.</strong> Efflorescence is mineral salt deposited as water evaporates out of the masonry surface. It is proof that water is travelling through the wall, and it comes back after every wipe-down until the movement is stopped.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Walls that feel cool and slightly damp.</strong> Especially at the base, and especially after a wet spell. Cardboard boxes stored against the wall going soft is the same signal, noticed sooner.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Paint or coatings peeling from the inside face.</strong> Vapour pressure pushing outwards lifts anything applied to the surface, which is why ordinary paint on a below-grade wall rarely survives more than a couple of years.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rust on anything metal stored against the wall.</strong> Shelf brackets, tool boxes and appliance feet corroding faster than they should is a quiet indicator of persistently high humidity coming off the masonry.
        </p>

        <h2 id="which-side" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Which Side to Treat
        </h2>

        <p>
          Exterior is better engineering and interior is what most people can actually do, and being honest about that trade-off is more useful than pretending otherwise.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The case for exterior.</strong> Water is stopped before it enters the wall at all. The membrane is pressed against the foundation by the very soil pressure that would push an interior coating off. The masonry never gets saturated, so freeze-thaw damage inside the wall stops as well. Combined with a drainage board and a working footing drain, this is the genuine, long-term solution, and our <a href="/waterproofing-basement-walls-from-outside/">exterior waterproofing guide</a> walks through the full build-up.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The case for interior.</strong> It costs a fraction as much, it can be done over a weekend, and for the most common problem — moisture migrating through porous masonry, showing as damp walls, musty air and efflorescence — it genuinely works. What it cannot do is stop water arriving in volume, because an interior coating is the last thing in the water's path and the least able to resist.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical rule: if an excavator is on site for any reason, seal the outside while the wall is exposed. If not, treat the inside and be realistic that you are managing seepage rather than defeating a drainage failure. Our <a href="/interior-vs-exterior-basement-waterproofing/">interior vs exterior comparison</a> puts costs and disruption side by side.
        </p>

