                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dense poured concrete</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reactive penetrating sealer</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">RadonSeal</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall never fully dries</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Damp-tolerant elastomeric</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ames Blue Max</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water after every storm</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None — drainage work</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gutters, grading, perimeter drain</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Coverage is worth checking before you buy as well: cementitious coatings cover far less per gallon on rough block than the tin implies, often 75 to 100 square feet per coat, and both coats are mandatory. Elastomeric membranes go on thicker still and cover less again, which is the main reason they are usually reserved for the walls that genuinely need flexibility.
        </p>

        <p>
          The last row is the one worth reading twice. No sealer on this page, at any price, resists water arriving in volume through a failed drainage path. Sealing in that situation moves the problem rather than solving it, and often moves it somewhere less visible.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Preparation and Application
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Strip the wall.</strong> Interior coatings that bond chemically need bare masonry. Old paint, whitewash or a previous coating all have to come off with a masonry stripper or a grinder with dust extraction, and silica dust demands proper respiratory protection.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Brush efflorescence off dry.</strong> Wire brush and vacuum. Washing it dissolves the salts back into the wall, where they recrystallise later and can push a new coating off from behind.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Fill every crack first.</strong> Hydraulic cement in a chased-out V-groove for stable cracks; polyurethane injection for an actively leaking crack in poured concrete. Let repairs cure fully. Our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">crack repair guide</a> covers which is which.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Work the first coat in.</strong> With cementitious products the first coat is brushed hard into the pores with a masonry brush — rolling bridges them and leaves pinholes. The second coat, 24 hours later, can be rolled and should cross the first so misses are visible.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>On the exterior, protect the membrane.</strong> A cured membrane can be damaged by sharp backfill. A drainage board over it does two jobs at once: it shields the coating and gives water a path down to the footing drain instead of pressing against the wall.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Block Walls vs Poured Walls</h3>
        <p>
          The two common foundation types behave differently enough that the same product can be right on one and wrong on the other.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Concrete block</strong> is hollow and coarse. Water entering the wall can travel vertically inside the cores, which means moisture that enters high can emerge low, often at the bottom course or the wall-floor joint — and that migration makes a purely surface diagnosis misleading. Block also has mortar joints, which are frequently the weakest path. Cementitious coatings suit block well because they fill the open pore structure; reactive silicates do far less, because coarse block does not give the reaction enough to work with.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Poured concrete</strong> is denser and has no cores, so moisture movement is slower and more uniform, but it cracks in a way block does not — vertical shrinkage cracks from the original cure are extremely common and are the usual leak path. Poured walls are where reactive penetrating sealers perform best, and where polyurethane crack injection is the correct repair rather than a surface patch.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Stone and rubble foundations</strong> in older houses are a third case entirely: irregular, full of mortar joints, and often built without any damp proof course. They rarely take a modern coating well, and the realistic approach is usually managing the moisture — drainage, ventilation and dehumidification — rather than trying to seal the wall as if it were a flat block surface.
        </p>

        <h2 id="drainage-first" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. What to Fix Before You Seal Anything
        </h2>

        <p>
          Three cheap jobs solve a surprising share of wet foundations, and all of them belong before a single tin is opened.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Gutters.</strong> A blocked gutter delivers the entire roof's runoff straight down the wall. Clearing them costs nothing and changes the volume of water reaching the foundation more than any coating can.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Downspouts.</strong> Discharge should be at least six feet from the house, ideally further. A downspout dumping at the foundation is a hose pointed at the wall every time it rains.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Grade.</strong> Soil should fall away from the house — a common target is six inches over the first ten feet. Settled beds and patios that slope back towards the wall are among the most frequent causes of a suddenly damp basement in an old house.
        </p>

        <p>
          Do those three, watch through one wet season, and then decide what sealing the wall needs. A great many foundations turn out to need much less than the owner feared, and our <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/">wet basement solutions guide</a> orders every remaining option by cost.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Do About a Crawl Space</h3>
        <p>
          Crawl spaces get asked about in the same breath as foundations and want a different answer. The walls can be treated with the same interior coatings, but the dominant moisture source in most crawl spaces is not the walls at all — it is the exposed soil floor, which evaporates water into the space continuously.
        </p>
        <p>
          The standard fix there is encapsulation: a heavy polyethylene vapour barrier laid over the ground, lapped and sealed at the seams, run up the walls and fastened, with a dehumidifier to handle what remains. Coating the walls without addressing the floor treats a fraction of the problem and rarely changes the humidity reading enough to notice. If the crawl space smells and the joists above show surface mould, start with the ground cover rather than a tin of sealer.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Before Fixing the Water Source</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gutters, downspouts and grading come first, always. A coating applied while the roof is still discharging against the wall is treating a symptom at considerable expense.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Builder Waterproofed It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most new foundations get damp proofing, not waterproofing — a thin asphalt spray rated for vapour rather than pressure, which degrades over decades. Finding damp on a relatively modern house does not mean something failed; it means the original spec was lighter than assumed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Paint or Efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cementitious sealers bond by reacting with bare masonry. Anything in between prevents that, and the coating lifts in sheets within a season. Stripping is the job; painting is the easy part.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfilling Straight Onto a Fresh Membrane</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sharp fill damages an exterior coating as it goes in, and every puncture is a path for water. A drainage board protects the membrane and relieves pressure at the same time — do not skip it to save a few dollars per foot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a Structural Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horizontal cracking, stair-stepping through mortar joints, or a wall that bows is a structural question. Coating over it hides the evidence while the underlying problem continues. Get it assessed before anything covers it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best foundation sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It depends on the side you can reach. Exterior during an excavation: a water-based elastomeric membrane like Liquid Rubber's foundation sealant. From inside: DRYLOK Extreme for damp block or poured walls, Ames Blue Max where the wall cracks, and RadonSeal on dense poured concrete.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Inside or outside?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Outside is better engineering — it stops water before it enters the wall and uses soil pressure to hold the membrane on. Inside is what most owners can do without excavating, and it genuinely works against seepage and damp, just not against water arriving in volume.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Interior DIY is mostly materials — typically several hundred dollars of coating for a basement perimeter, plus preparation tools. Exterior means excavation, which puts a professional job into the thousands, with the sealer itself a small fraction of the total.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does interior sealing actually work?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For moisture migrating through porous masonry, yes — two coats of a cementitious waterproofer on bare, prepared walls reliably stops the damp, musty, efflorescent case. It will not stop water entering through a failed drain, a cracked wall or a rising water table.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">An exterior membrane buried and protected from UV is effectively permanent for the life of the backfill. Interior cementitious coatings commonly exceed a decade, with DRYLOK Extreme warranted for 15 years. Reactive penetrating sealers do not wear at all. All of that assumes correct preparation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do cracks need fixing first?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — coatings seal pores, not gaps. Fill with hydraulic cement, or inject an actively leaking crack in poured concrete with polyurethane, and let the repair cure before coating. Wide, stepped or moving cracks should be assessed structurally first.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Damp proofing or waterproofing — what is the difference?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Damp proofing resists vapour and light seepage; waterproofing is rated against liquid water under hydrostatic pressure. The asphalt spray on many new foundations is damp proofing, which is why a relatively modern house can still show damp without anything having failed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing stop a wet basement?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Only if water is seeping through the masonry itself. If it arrives after heavy rain, pools on the floor, or shows at the wall-floor joint, the answer is drainage — gutters and grading first, then a perimeter drain and sump. Sealing before those is money on a symptom.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

